The `==` operator in Java is used to compare references (memory addresses) of objects, not their values. Therefore, when comparing strings using `==`, you are checking whether two string objects refer to the same instance in memory, rather than comparing their contents.

On the other hand, the `.equals()` method is a more appropriate and recommended way to compare the contents (value) of strings in Java. This method is defined in the `java.lang.String` class and compares the characters of two strings, ignoring any differences in case.

Using `==` to compare strings can lead to unexpected behavior and potential bugs, especially when dealing with string literals or when working with mutable strings (e.g., `StringBuffer`, `StringBuilder`). 

To summarize, you should use the `.equals()` method instead of `==` when comparing strings in Java. Use `==` only when you specifically want to compare references, such as when checking for null values or when comparing string objects explicitly created with `new` operator.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";

// Using == operator
if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 refer to the same instance");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are different instances");
}

// Using equals() method
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have the same contents");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have different contents");
}
``` 

